At the talks, delegations from 139 countries met in plenary sessions for one week before dividing up into smaller groups to discuss more detailed proposals.

The meeting was supposed to come to a close yesterday (7 December), but with little progress made so far, it could go on for days.

Developing countries such as China, Brazil, India and South Africa have been resisting any tightening of international patent laws. They argue that tighter IP protection would drive up drug prices at a time when millions are dying because they can't afford life-saving treatments.

China has said that it will use its growing influence in the WTO to block any further moves towards stronger IP protection.

The US wants a new global IP treaty to include:

- compulsory licensing of patented drugs only under exceptional circumstances;
- data exclusivity for clinical trials of new drugs;
- longer periods of patent protection;
- criminal sanctions against counterfeiters, including prison sentences;
- measures for border control for counterfeits.

