This is one of the main recommendations of a new report by the Commission for Social Justice, a group of academics and activists that includes Diane Abbott MP and Paul Sagar from LSE Cities.

The report sets out an ambitious alternative agenda for government and suggests what steps the next Labour Government could take to make a difference to people’s lives. It calls on the next Labour Government to:

- Create an independent national authority to acquire land at no more than cost and dispose it on the same basis. This would end the perverse tax incentives and public subsidies that reward those who hold onto land and those who speculate on the price of land;
- Reform the planning system so that landowners are compensated according to the most likely use of the land under the plans of the local authority;
- Introduce rent controls;
- Allow councils to borrow against future receipts from council house sales;
- Give tenants of all social housing a right to buy their homes after five years, which will encourage councils to build more houses for rent;
- Double the proportion of council housing.

