Rent Control in NYC: An Illustrated Guide examines the effects of rent control on housing, from its origins as a wartime measure in 1943 to present-day New York City, which has some of the strictest rent regulations in the country. Despite New York State’s rent regulations being among the most stringent in the nation, rent-stabilized units have become concentrated in high-demand neighborhoods such as Manhattan, while other areas face depleted stock. Meanwhile, the average cost of a studio apartment in the city is $2,056—the highest in the country by far.

This report provides an overview of rent control policies across the country and throughout history, and examines their impact on New York, including:

- How rent control leads to shortages of low-cost rental units;
- Why rent control does not prevent gentrification or displacement;
- Why rent control fails to protect renters from eviction;
- How rent control can disincentivize homeownership; and
- The economic fallacies and unintended consequences of rent control.

